        "comment": {
          "type": "comment",
          "settings": {
            "avatar_asset": "pawsorb-v2-avatar.jpg",
            "name": "Tasha Greene",
            "text": "<p>Sunday night, I turned around to grab the pizza from the oven and the grape bowl on the coffee table was empty. Two dogs, no idea which one ate them. Pearl is 25 pounds, so one tube dialed to 25. Rocky is 70, so three tubes. Gave both right there on the rug, then called the vet. Both fine by bedtime. Learn from me: it's 1 mL per pound and a tube holds 30, so count tubes for every dog in the house, not just one.</p>",
            "reactions": "214",
            "heart_color": "#E5484D",
            "meta": "Like · Reply · 6d"
          }
        },
        "faq_1": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What if I'm not sure what they swallowed?",
            "answer": "<p>Give it anyway. It's gentle enough to give on a hunch, and nothing gets forced back up. Waiting to see if symptoms show is how the first hour slips away.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_2": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How do I give it with shaking hands?",
            "answer": "<p>Turn the dial until it shows your pet's weight. Slip the tip into the cheek pouch, behind the back teeth, and press. No powder, no mixing, no math. Done in under 60 seconds.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_3": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is it okay for a 6 lb puppy or my cat?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. The dial doses to the pound, so a 6 lb puppy gets 6 mL and a 10 lb cat gets 10. Cats get the same dial as dogs, with nothing left to guess.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_4": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Isn't hydrogen peroxide what you're supposed to use?",
            "answer": "<p>That's old internet advice. Peroxide makes a dog vomit, which can burn the throat, inflame the stomach and send vomit into the lungs. PawSorb binds the poison instead, and nothing comes back up.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_5": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How soon do I need to give it?",
            "answer": "<p>Right away, before any symptoms. Most poisons soak into the blood within 60 minutes, toads in 15 to 30. That's why it lives in your drawer: there's no time to order one mid-scare.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_6": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How long does it keep, and where should it live?",
            "answer": "<p>Two years, sealed, at room temperature. Keep one in the kitchen drawer, one in the car or the dog bag, and one at Grandma's if your dog stays there.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_7": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "When will it arrive, and can I return it?",
            "answer": "<p>It ships fast from the US with tracking. Shipping is free on 3 or more tubes, $4.99 below that. You have 90 days to send it back for a full refund.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
